WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.100
MICHAEL YU: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.730
First off, thanks
for coming today.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.740
I'm really excited to be here.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.050
My name is Michael Yu.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.450
I'm a software developer
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.309
And with me today
is James Malone.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.610
He's a product manager
on Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.320
So over the last
couple years, I've

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.960
had the opportunity to work
with a bunch of our users,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.642
and see some of the
issues that they run into.

00:00:26.642 --> 00:00:28.100
So today, what we
were hoping to do

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.610
was go over some of the
common issues that we see

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.720
and some of the
common misconceptions,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.880
as well as some of
the common solutions.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.251
And we're only going to
touch a handful of these.

00:00:37.251 --> 00:00:39.500
There's not enough time to
really cover a lot of them.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.105
But what we're also
hoping to do is

00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:43.730
give you a little bit of insight
in terms of how the service is

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.550
working, and why we made
certain design decisions that

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.236
can lead to certain problems.

00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:49.610
So hopefully, this
will be really

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.684
interesting and useful and fun.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.850
Here's the agenda for today.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.550
The talks are broken down
into four main sections.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.060
The first two sections
cover our primary use cases,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.610
which are data processing
and content serving.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.740
So for data processing
workloads and content serving,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.740
we're going to first talk
about storage classes.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.080
And the reason why is that
there's some misconceptions

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:09.960
around them.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.939
Particularly,
we've commonly seen

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:13.730
that some folks believe
that multi-regional

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.790
is a strictly superior
storage class to regional.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.690
And that's not true.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.310
There's actually
trade-offs between the two.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.476
And so we'll go into some
of the latency differences

00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:23.720
and some of the
consistency differences.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.450
We're also going to talk about
roughly what performance you

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.090
can expect out of GCS.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.712
And we're going to talk about
how to change your access

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:33.920
patterns to take advantage
of our relative strengths,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.820
and hopefully work around some
of our relative weaknesses.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.940
The third section is going
to be on write scaling.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.050
This is a problem that
we see fairly often.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.000
Folks will end up
hitting a bottleneck.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.680
And they're not able to scale
the number of writes per

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.715
second they can get.

00:01:46.715 --> 00:01:50.000
So we're going to take a look
at some of the design decisions

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.426
that we made that resulted
in this hot spotting.

00:01:52.426 --> 00:01:54.050
And we'll sort of
explain our rationale

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.530
for making those decisions.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.380
And then we'll of course talk
about ways to get around that.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.005
And then finally, we're going to
wrap up talking about retries.

00:02:01.005 --> 00:02:03.088
You know, retries is
probably something everyone's

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:03.740
familiar with.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.240
But there's different
strategies you

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.830
want to use when
dealing with errors

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.042
versus dealing with slow
requests and tail latency.

00:02:09.042 --> 00:02:11.000
So we're going to talk
about a technique called

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.360
hedge requests, which is a good
way to deal with tail latency.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:16.860
OK.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.410
So let's get to the
first section, which

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.985
is data processing workloads.

00:02:21.985 --> 00:02:24.360
Hopefully, you guys are all
familiar with storage classes

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:24.940
for GCS.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.930
But let me give a quick
overview for those who aren't.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.300
So we have four main
classes, multi-regional,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.630
regional, nearline and coldline.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.240
For the multi-regional storage
class, what we guarantee

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.530
is that you're going to
have at least two replicas.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.045
And they're going
to be geo diverse.

00:02:40.045 --> 00:02:41.670
So the replicas are
going to be located

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.290
at least 100 miles apart.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.260
So in this particular
example, we have four objects.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.380
There's a replica for each
of them in the left region.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.020
And then the second replicas
for the other four objects

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.190
are split between the
middle and the right region.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:56.750
The second source
class is regional.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.940
And here you select a
very specific region

00:02:58.940 --> 00:02:59.930
to store your data.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.510
And we guarantee
that all of your data

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.590
lies within that region.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.840
The last two source classes
are nearline and coldline.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.441
They're designed
for colder data.

00:03:08.441 --> 00:03:10.440
They're a little bit
cheaper to store your data.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.810
They cost a little bit
more to access your data.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.890
And in this example up here, the
main thing I wanted to call out

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.422
is that for nearline
and coldline,

00:03:18.422 --> 00:03:20.130
you can choose either
a regional location

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.810
or a multi-regional location.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.450
But they don't really
provide geo diversity

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.694
within a single object.

00:03:26.694 --> 00:03:28.110
So in this particular
case, object

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.100
one is in the left region.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.830
Object two is in
the middle region.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.371
Neither of those
objects are actually

00:03:33.371 --> 00:03:34.920
split between multiple regions.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.130
OK.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.254
So here's a table that
shows the relative strengths

00:03:37.254 --> 00:03:40.260
and weaknesses of our
various storage classes.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.000
And for data
processing workloads,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.430
I wanted to call
out two attributes,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.864
The first is local latency.

00:03:45.864 --> 00:03:47.280
Your data processing
workload, you

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.650
want to locate your compute
next to your storage.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.750
And regional has much
tighter local agency balance.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.559
This is actually maybe not
as obvious as it might seem.

00:03:57.559 --> 00:03:59.100
The reason why is
for multi-regional,

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.574
we have multiple replicas.

00:04:00.574 --> 00:04:02.490
And as long as a replica
is near your compute,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.110
you might think the
latency is going to be OK.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.480
Right?

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.080
But the reality is that for our
multi-regional storage class,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.910
you define a
overall multi-region

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.140
that we store your data in.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.574
Such as a US or Asia or EU.

00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:17.490
And you don't have really
fine grained control

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.790
over exactly which regions
within the multi region

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.980
that are used.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.880
And so even though
we do make an attempt

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.600
to keep your data in the region
that you initially wrote it in,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.980
over time it can migrate out.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.460
And that can result in
higher tail latency.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.344
The second reason that
local latency isn't as great

00:04:35.344 --> 00:04:36.510
is a little bit more subtle.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.680
And that has to do
with the write latency.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.180
So for the multi-regional
storage class, we of course

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.310
replicate your data to
two different locations.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.185
And we need to maintain
some type of metadata

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:48.220
that says where your
data is actually located.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.300
And this metadata has to be
replicated to multiple regions

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.350
as well, right?

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.340
If we didn't, then we're not
really providing a true fault

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.510
tolerance across regions.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.730
So that's one aspect.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.400
The other aspect is that
GCS provides strong read

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.570
after write consistency.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.610
So that means if
I write an object,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.920
we guarantee that
every read afterwards

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.960
is going to see
this latest version.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:18.010
So when you look at those
two pieces combined,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:25.590
what that implies
is that you're going

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.060
to have to syncronize this to
replicate your metadata out.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.601
And so every write
is going to result

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:32.430
in at least one remote round
trip out of the region.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.230
And so no matter how
much we optimize,

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.095
you're going to be bound
by that network latency.

00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:39.470
So local agency
is one big reason

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.510
why you want to use regional
for data processing.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.593
The other reason is actually
something pretty cool

00:05:43.593 --> 00:05:45.710
that we haven't announced
I think until now.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.380
And that is for the regional
storage class, as well

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.220
as any nearline or coldline
that uses a regional location,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.700
we're now guaranteeing strong
list after write consistency.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.820
So this means that if
you write an object

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.900
and then you immediately
list your objects,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.774
the newly written object
is going to show up.

00:06:01.774 --> 00:06:02.530
We guarantee that.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.510
And if you delete an object
and then list all your objects,

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:07.940
that object was
guaranteed to not show up.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.280
So now all of a
sudden, your workloads

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.250
can really rely on this
strong consistency,

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.810
which can be really useful.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:16.880
And we don't yet have
this for multi-regional.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.500
So is a pretty strong reason
to go ahead and use regional.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.090
Next I wanted to
talk a little bit

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.460
about some of the performance
characteristics of GCS.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:27.680
These are really, really
rough rules of thumb.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.100
I essentially tried to make the
first number be powers of 2.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.410
But they're relatively accurate.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.270
And the thing to
notice here is that GCS

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:39.050
is relatively strong in terms
of single stream throughput.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:41.600
We think we usually
roughly around 800 megabits

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.260
per second for reads, 400
megabits per second for writes.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.210
On the flip side,
we're relatively

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.040
weak in terms of the fixed
latency cost per request.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.040
And so a rough rule of
thumb is 100 milliseconds

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.260
for read, 200
milliseconds for writes.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.390
So there's a couple
of implications here.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:00.320
So first is obviously favor
larger requests, right?

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.600
Take advantage of
that throughput.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:08.410
Second of all, if you
do have small requests,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.230
parallelize them, right?

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:14.040
So that way, you're really
overlapping that fixed cost,

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.820
fixed latency cost.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.100
And then finally,
this is a little bit

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.740
subtle, but in general for
data processing workloads,

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.750
you want to avoid
resumable uploads.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.190
Especially for small requests.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.820
And the definition of small is
somewhat surprising sometimes.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.520
Does that mean five
megabytes or 10 megabytes?

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:32.930
Maybe 100 megabytes?

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.720
Well, if you look at our 400
megabits per second write,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.510
that's equivalent to 50
megabytes per second.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.326
If you have a one
gigabyte size object,

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:42.200
that means that a 1
gigabyte sized object can

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.460
be written in about 20 seconds.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.660
So if you're willing to give
up to 20 seconds of work

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.790
in the rare case that
you encounter an error,

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.570
then you actually don't really
need to be using resumable.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:56.005
And the reason why we
don't recommend resumable

00:07:56.005 --> 00:07:58.130
for these small requests
is that a resumable upload

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.040
requires at least two
round trips to the server.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.800
The first round trip
establishes a unique session

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:07.020
that you then use in your second
round trip to upload your data.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.520
And again, because we had
this fixed latency cost,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.920
that's going to
add up over time.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:16.290
I actually wanted to mention
kind of an interesting story.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.810
We actually ran
into this ourselves

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.650
once, where we were doing some
benchmarking using test DFS IO.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.170
What this does is essentially
spins up a bunch of workers,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.930
and it does a bunch of writes
and does a bunch of reads.

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.880
So it's a very simple I/O test.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.310
And when we ran
this benchmark, it

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.309
was taking a little bit
longer than we expected.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:34.409
So we dug in.

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:36.299
And the reading and
the writing portion

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.820
was actually quite quick.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.270
It was sort of scaling
as we expected.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.250
However, the job was taking
a long time to start up.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.040
So when we dug into that
even further, what we noticed

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.400
is that the controller was
writing these small control

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.860
files that coordinate
all the workers.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:56.440
And it was running these small
files serially, one by one.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:57.940
It was also using
resumable uploads,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.210
so that meant each file
actually required two round

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.780
trips to the server.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.660
So if you kind of do
the rough napkin math,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.720
we're talking about 400
milliseconds per control file,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.782
maybe 2.5 control
files per second.

00:09:08.782 --> 00:09:10.740
You're talking about
spinning up 1,000 workers,

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.810
you're already talking
about 400 seconds

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.316
or about six or seven minutes,
just to start up the job.

00:09:16.316 --> 00:09:17.940
So in hindsight, it
was pretty obvious.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.650
But it's kind of a
lesson where if you're

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.450
taking a workload from
an existing system

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.960
and you're moving it to GCS, the
performance characteristics can

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:25.710
be different.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.170
And so you want to
take that into account.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:31.290
And make sure you're
parallelizing.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:31.900
OK.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:34.240
Up next, James is going
to do a quick demo

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.480
on Cloud Dataproc that
should hopefully show some

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:38.770
of these principles in play.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.092
SPEAKER 1: Excellent.

00:09:40.092 --> 00:09:41.300
Thank you all for being here.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:42.889
It's nice to see all of you.

00:09:42.889 --> 00:09:43.930
So I'm a product manager.

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:46.120
And I focus on Google
Cloud Dataproc.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.480
Dataproc is our managed
Spark and Hadoop offering.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.850
And the demo I'm going to
show you is really important.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:53.770
Because one of the
principal things that we

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:55.900
try to do with Dataproc
and many of our products

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.690
is really separate
storage and compute.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:01.150
And with Spark and Hadoop,
usually the storage

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:03.010
is intrinsically tied
to your clusters.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.600
So often with Spark
and Hadoop, you

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.562
have the Hadoop Distributed
File System, or HDFS.

00:10:07.562 --> 00:10:09.020
That often lives
with your cluster.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.103
And a lot of times, you
have to scale your cluster

00:10:11.103 --> 00:10:12.100
to store data.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.740
And Hadoop has tried to
solve many of the hard things

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.870
that GCS solves behind
the scenes for you.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:21.630
Like replication, like
performance, durability.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:23.710
By being able to use
Google Cloud Storage,

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.960
you can have your data
live in Google Cloud,

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:30.310
and use it with Dataproc or
any multiple other products,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.350
and you Spark and Hadoop
on that data as you wish.

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:35.770
So the demo I'm going to
show you today is twofold.

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.350
One, I'm going to show you a
Spark streaming example that

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.330
takes advantage of
the list consistency

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:42.120
that's built into
regional buckets.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.056
What's really cool about that
is soon as we're writing files

00:10:45.056 --> 00:10:46.930
to this cloud storage
bucket, Spark Streaming

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:49.000
is picking them up
and processing them.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.560
That's often a very
difficult thing

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.080
to do with cloud environments
and block stores.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:56.590
And the second is, as Mike
said, some of the numbers

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.450
we showed you, that's great.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:59.800
I love showing numbers.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.690
I'm a big fan of
actually showing

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.400
you the numbers in real time.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.607
So I wanted to substantiate
some of the performance

00:11:05.607 --> 00:11:07.690
that we're seeing, and
talk about how we're really

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.200
trying to enhance performance
with Cloud Dataproc and cloud

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.120
storage.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.190
So the first thing
I'm going to do here--

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.170
and I'll come back to this--

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:21.190
I'm going to submit a job
against two Spark clusters.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.340
These jobs are going to test
a change that we recently

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.650
made that I'll talk about
that demonstrates some

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:29.530
of the performance enhancements
and underlying performance

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:31.750
characteristics
of cloud storage.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:33.280
These are Hadoop jobs.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:34.990
And behind the
scenes, I'm actually

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:38.710
just running test DFS IO to sort
of link the stories together

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:39.710
here.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.920
And we're going to read 20 files
that are 1000 megabytes apiece.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.950
So I'm going to go ahead and
just kick off these two jobs.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.570
With Cloud Dataproc
we have a control

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.939
plain to submit work to
Spark and Hadoop clusters.

00:11:52.939 --> 00:11:55.480
You don't have to go in and use
the really complicated Hadoop

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.180
tools.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:02.140
And of course that
just is problematic.

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:06.190
The next thing I'm going
to do is run through a demo

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:07.360
to show you Spark streaming.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:11.320
And some of the advantages
of using consistency that's

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.420
built into regional buckets.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.160
So I have two clusters here.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.200
I'm going to go ahead and
create these clusters.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.610
And these clusters are going
to go and actually read data

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.580
from my cloud storage bucket.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:27.610
And they're in the
process of creating.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.680
And I'll actually
show you, we generally

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.390
can create Spark
and Hadoop clusters

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.215
in like 60 to 90 seconds.

00:12:33.215 --> 00:12:35.215
Which is why I'm totally
comfortable standing up

00:12:35.215 --> 00:12:37.930
here and creating Spark
and Hadoop clusters.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.174
In the demo here--

00:12:39.174 --> 00:12:40.840
and you can see this
on the very bottom.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:44.020
And I apologize, it's
a one line shellscript.

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.210
But I want to show you
exactly what we're doing.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:50.170
We're going to copy data
from one bucket to another.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.950
And if it's somewhat not
obvious by the script here,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:57.540
I've downloaded a bunch of books
from the Rittenberg collection.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.100
So these are text files
that range from like 10

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.434
kilobytes to 4 megabytes.

00:13:02.434 --> 00:13:03.850
The goal here is
to show you, this

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.350
is typical kind of log data.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:06.910
Like it's going to
be variable size,

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.280
it's not going to be huge.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.690
And it takes advantage
of some of the things

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:13.300
that we would recommend if
you're using Spark and Hadoop

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:16.080
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.850
Ultimately, I'm going to
copy these files kind of one

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:22.480
at a time, just to show you that
streaming against these files

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.530
works.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:25.660
I'm sleeping for eight
seconds with the cluster,

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.300
just so you can
actually see the output.

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:31.570
Unfortunately, Google Cloud
Storage and Cloud Dataproc

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.910
are so fast that if
I did it all at once,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:35.560
you'd just see a
big blur of text.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:36.664
And it looks cool.

00:13:36.664 --> 00:13:38.830
And it looks like your job
is working at warp speed.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:40.519
But it's really hard to see.

00:13:40.519 --> 00:13:42.310
So what we're going to
do is we'll kick off

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.320
this Spark streaming job.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.830
And the Spark streaming job--

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:47.860
and if you don't believe
me, the file name

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:49.270
is actually even the same--

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:52.210
this is just a canonical
Spark streaming example.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.550
It's actually from the
Spark documentation.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:57.490
And it's just reading
data from HDFS.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.200
Or so Spark thinks.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.405
So we have a cloud storage
connector for Spark and Hadoop

00:14:02.405 --> 00:14:04.450
that lives on Cloud Dataproc.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.240
And it can read and
write from cloud storage.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.460
And assumes it's HDFS.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.800
And the reason is that we
implement the Hadoop file

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.860
system APIs so we can
interact with cloud storage

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:18.620
as if it were HDFS that's
attached to the cluster itself.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:20.410
So we're going to
run this word count.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.270
And it's just running
against bucket,

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:26.160
which is a regional bucket
that has strong consistency.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:27.960
These two clusters
that I created,

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:32.280
next demo one and next demo two,
are in two different regions.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:33.900
And the reason
that I did that was

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:36.120
to show you that we
can actually have

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:41.520
two clusters reading from the
same bucket at the same time.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:42.720
And this is really powerful.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.080
Because we're actually operating
with two different Spark

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:49.140
and Hadoop clusters against
one cloud storage bucket

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:52.380
at the very same time in two
completely different regions.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.540
The first cluster is
inside of the US region.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.720
And the second is
inside of the EU region.

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.160
And we're reading
a data bucket that

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:01.140
is inside of the US region.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:03.480
So it's actually
really neat to see

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.880
that this just kind
of magically works.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.190
So you can see
here, I've already

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:09.510
started streaming files.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:12.630
And actually this will be a bit
more meaningful here if we go

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:16.800
and go back to our-- let's
look at the jobs here.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:24.769
So as we're streaming
data to this bucket,

00:15:24.769 --> 00:15:26.560
you can see that we're
basically performing

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:27.730
word counts in real time.

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.700
And I'm actually getting the
real time output of these files

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.137
as they're being written.

00:15:32.137 --> 00:15:34.720
If we actually make this Cloud
Shell bigger-- and I apologize,

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:38.500
this is just the unfortunate
side effect of presenting

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:39.830
on a very large screen--

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:42.820
so we're actually just copying
these files over one at a time,

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.620
and putting them in that bucket.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.630
As soon as they
appear in that bucket,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.060
they're being instantly
picked up and processed

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:50.260
by Cloud Dataproc.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:53.110
So when you saw the console,
you saw that we had just

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:53.750
written a file.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.170
Here's the word
count of that file.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.960
And every second Spark
is looking for new files.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:01.570
And it's doing a word
count on them instantly.

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:03.400
There's not magic here.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:04.900
It's not complicated.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:06.970
Literally, all we're
doing is putting

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:09.610
what is a substitute for
log data in a bucket,

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.890
and our Spark and Hadoop
clusters are immediately

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:15.120
able to process that data.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.720
If we look at the first
job that I kicked off,

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:21.360
the other thing I want to
share with you is we've

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.000
made improvements to our
cloud storage connector

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.740
for Spark and Hadoop.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.500
So we read 20 files that
were 1,000 megabytes.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.640
Our average I/O throughput
was 217 megabytes per second.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.680
If we translate that
to megabits per second,

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.010
we're looking at well over
1,600 megabits per second.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.526
So 800 may be a great rule.

00:16:41.526 --> 00:16:42.900
Your mileage may
vary, of course,

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.430
depending on your workloads.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:46.530
But in general,
this is to show you

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.097
that when we say
it's performant,

00:16:48.097 --> 00:16:49.680
we're not just saying
it's performant.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.790
It really is performant.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:55.740
All of these examples will also
be on our GitHub repository,

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.680
just in case you want to run
through them and test them.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.200
So as I talked about,
there's a few best practices

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:02.460
that we'd recommend.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:06.390
If consistency matters,
use regional buckets.

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:09.510
We recommend generally that you
keep your clusters and buckets

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:10.900
close together.

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:13.020
And the reason for
that is if you're

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.970
doing things like
stream processing,

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:16.230
milliseconds can matter.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.750
And the closer that
all of your operations

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.190
are occurring to each
other, the better.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:23.910
If you have a lot of
operations, parallelize them.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:26.040
Because as I showed, you
can read from one bucket

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:26.967
at the same time.

00:17:26.967 --> 00:17:28.800
You can have multiple
clusters parallelized,

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.340
or you can have
jobs parallelized.

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.070
We update Cloud Dataproc and
our cloud storage connector

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:36.840
for Spark and Hadoop frequently.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.270
The SSL improvements that
you saw where we're actually

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:42.270
reading at 1,600
megabits per second

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:45.100
is rolling out in
the next week or two.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:48.040
If you are using Spark
and Hadoop, 8 cores per VM

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.230
will give you the maximum
performance, just based

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:52.900
on our long testing.

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:56.170
And don't use wildcards
in silly ways.

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:58.120
So whenever you do
a wildcard, you're

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.430
going to get a list
of the entire bucket.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.692
Don't try and list
a large bucket

00:18:02.692 --> 00:18:04.900
if you only need to operate
on a subset of the files.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:07.191
Because you're going to get
back way more data than you

00:18:07.191 --> 00:18:08.500
actually need to operate.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.420
And with that, I will
turn it back over.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:11.200
Thanks.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.420
MICHAEL YU: Thanks, James.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:13.920
OK.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.240
Let's move on to
content serving.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:18.140
So this is any
use case where you

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:19.640
have contents where
in GCS, you want

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:22.320
to distribute it to users
who are potentially all

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:24.600
over the globe.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:26.034
I showed a slide previously.

00:18:26.034 --> 00:18:27.450
The one thing I
wanted to call out

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:30.270
is multi-regional
can be a good source

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:31.980
class to use in this case.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:33.480
The primary reason
is that you get

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:36.062
better remote latency due
to having multiple replicas.

00:18:36.062 --> 00:18:37.770
And of course you get
better availability

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:40.470
due to having geo diversity.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.420
And here, things
like list consistency

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:43.920
don't typically matter as much.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:50.110
So one of the
things that I think

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:53.320
not everyone knows about us is
that we have this pretty nice

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:54.740
global network.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:57.240
We do edge caching,
we do core caching.

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.740
And so what does
that actually mean?

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:00.389
So without any of
this, typically

00:19:00.389 --> 00:19:01.930
what would happen
when a user fetches

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:04.720
a object is the
request would traverse

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.100
across the public internet.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.940
And it would eventually
hit the region

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:09.790
that the object's located in.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:12.080
It would ingress at the
Google network at that region.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:15.250
We would serve the object and
the response would flow back

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.040
over the public internet.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.170
So what GCS actually does is
Google has points of presence

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:22.040
all over the globe.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:24.710
And we try to get the users'
requests onto our nearest

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:25.624
point of presence.

00:19:25.624 --> 00:19:27.290
And once it's on that
point of presence,

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:30.320
we terminate the request,
and we forward it internally

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:31.960
over our private network.

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:34.514
And so a longer
portion of the request

00:19:34.514 --> 00:19:35.930
is over a private
network where we

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.190
have tighter control over
the quality of service

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:39.110
we can provide.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:41.750
So that's a pretty nice benefit.

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:44.120
We also end up doing
automatic failover.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:45.710
So we'll find the
nearest replica.

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.625
In this particular
case, it's in region A.

00:19:47.625 --> 00:19:50.180
And if region A isn't healthy,
we'll fall back to region B

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.180
automatically.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.280
The third thing
that's nice about this

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:56.020
is we have, like I
mentioned earlier,

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:59.030
edge caching, and caching in
our core data centers as well.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:01.120
So for hot content,
subsequent requests

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:02.830
can be served
directly from cache

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.660
rather than having
to travel, say,

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.080
halfway around the world
to the original source.

00:20:09.792 --> 00:20:12.000
Some of you guys might be
familiar with our Cloud CDN

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.375
product and might
be saying, hey,

00:20:13.375 --> 00:20:16.090
this sounds really
familiar or very similar.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:17.141
And that's exactly right.

00:20:17.141 --> 00:20:19.515
We actually use the same global
network and the same edge

00:20:19.515 --> 00:20:21.850
caching infrastructure
as Cloud CDN.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:23.720
So really, a lot of
Cloud CDN functionality

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.900
is built straight
into GCS without you

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.742
having to do anything.

00:20:27.742 --> 00:20:29.950
There are also some good
reasons for using Cloud CDN.

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.530
In particular, if you want to
set up SSL for custom domains.

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:35.110
Or you want to customize
your URL structure.

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:39.710
Perhaps point a subset of URLs
at GCS, and a subset of URLs

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:41.620
at your compute
instances, you would

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:45.010
want to use Cloud CDN or
a Cloud Load Balancer.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.720
It also provides
differentiated pricing.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.210
So some requests are
served out of cache.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.714
Some requests are served
from cache misses.

00:20:51.714 --> 00:20:53.380
And if you want
different pricing there,

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.640
you can use cloud CDN.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:59.760
So next, I'm going to do a quick
demo to sort of demonstrate

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.480
what I mean by this
global network.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:05.240
So over here, you can see
I've created a demo bucket.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:08.130
You can see it's
multi-regional storage class.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:09.740
And it's located in the EU.

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:12.300
So it's a pretty good
distance away from here

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.084
in San Francisco.

00:21:14.084 --> 00:21:16.000
I'm going to switch over
to the terminal here.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.970
And the first thing
I'll do is I'll

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:20.610
ping our bucket right here.

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:24.345
So io321
demo.storage.googleapis.com.

00:21:24.345 --> 00:21:25.970
And what you can see
here is that we're

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:29.300
getting a round trip time
of about three milliseconds.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.340
So clearly, it's not
going to Europe, right?

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:33.860
That's maybe 150, 200
milliseconds away.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:38.090
So the request is getting
terminated somewhere close.

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:42.800
Let's actually go ahead
and perform a trace route.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:44.720
And what a trace rout
does, is it shows you

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.610
all the hops that
a request takes

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.460
towards its final destination.

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:52.470
And so you can ignore the
first 11 or so entries here.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.100
The important one here
is this very last entry.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:58.400
Nuq, something,
something, 1e100.net.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:01.610
So what is that?

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:04.160
First of all, I'm sure a lot
of you guys already know this.

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:06.770
But Google itself was
actually misnamed.

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:09.770
It was supposed
to be G-O-O-G-O-L.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:13.180
So if we look up the
definition of that,

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:16.580
it's 1 times 10 to the 100.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:18.731
And that's exactly
what 1e100 is.

00:22:18.731 --> 00:22:20.480
It's our little clever
way of saying, hey,

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.409
this is a Google device.

00:22:22.409 --> 00:22:23.950
If you look at the
rest of the name--

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:26.500
and we're not going to go
into too many of the details--

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:28.060
you can see nuq.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:30.440
And you might
wonder what that is.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:33.220
So if we do a quick
search for that,

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:37.080
it looks like it's
Moffett Federal Airfield,

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:38.410
here in the Bay Area.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:42.910
So you might guess that request
is getting terminated somewhere

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:43.946
close by here.

00:22:43.946 --> 00:22:45.320
We have a point
of presence here.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:47.190
And that's where we're
handling the request.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:50.370
And once it gets here, we're
going to go on to Europe.

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:51.790
Right?

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:56.620
OK, so next, I've uploaded
an object into this bucket.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:02.050
And we can perform a
curl to pull the object.

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:05.020
And we can time it to
see how long it'll take.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:08.690
So here you can see I'm curling,
I'm just silencing the output.

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:11.950
I'm riding the
output to dev null.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:15.410
And I'm pulling
this foo.bin object.

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:15.910
All right.

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:18.380
Let's see what happens.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.380
So you can see, it's taking
about 200 milliseconds.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:22.260
And that makes sense.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:24.010
It's got to traverse
all the way to Europe

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.870
to get this thing, right?

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:32.430
So how do we actually
enable this edge caching?

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:37.410
What we can do is if you go
into your storage browser,

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:42.130
we can edit the data
properties of the object.

00:23:47.654 --> 00:23:49.820
Here you can see that one
of the metadata properties

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:51.510
is cache control header.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:53.100
And it's being set to private.

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:57.215
So if we wipe this
out, by default GCS

00:23:57.215 --> 00:23:59.580
will mark your object
as publicly cacheable,

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.335
assuming your object
is actually public.

00:24:01.335 --> 00:24:03.210
If it's private, it
won't do this, of course.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.251
So I'm going to just go
ahead and clear this out.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:08.090
OK.

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:10.850
So now if I go back and
rerun that curl command

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:15.150
to pull the object, previously
it was about 200 milliseconds.

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:18.390
We're now seeing about
20-ish milliseconds,

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:19.920
20 to 30 milliseconds.

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:22.860
So that goes to show that the
object's now cached closer

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:23.682
to the edge.

00:24:23.682 --> 00:24:25.390
And it's being served
straight from that.

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:28.620
So it's a pretty
simple technique.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:30.060
Obviously just enable caching.

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:31.435
But it's one of
the things that I

00:24:31.435 --> 00:24:34.540
think is not always well
known, is that we have this CDN

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:35.500
functionality built in.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.874
And if you pair it with the
multi-regional storage class,

00:24:37.874 --> 00:24:40.950
you're talking
about geo diversity,

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.980
CDN-like functionality,
all sort of in one product

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.470
without having to do too
much additional set up.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:51.680
That said, we still
definitely recommend

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:52.900
using independent CDNs.

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:54.410
There's nice benefits to that.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:57.470
They have different
reaches than Google has.

00:24:57.470 --> 00:24:59.677
So besides having
that additional reach,

00:24:59.677 --> 00:25:01.510
it's nice having that
additional redundancy.

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:03.660
And it also allows caching
of private content.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:06.250
So if you use a CDN, one
of the things we recommend

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:07.850
is a seven origin shield.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:11.270
What that does is normally
when you have a cache miss,

00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:14.390
all of your edge nodes are going
to fetch the data so that they

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.880
can fill their cache, right?

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.974
An origin shield is
an intermediate cache.

00:25:18.974 --> 00:25:21.140
Where your edge nodes can
talk to the origin shield,

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:23.150
the origin shield
can fetch your data,

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:25.694
and then it can serve that data
back out to your edge nodes.

00:25:25.694 --> 00:25:27.110
And this is really
nice especially

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:29.561
if using coldline or nearline,
where the access charges

00:25:29.561 --> 00:25:30.560
are a little bit higher.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:33.560
That origin shield allows
all of your edge nodes

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:37.137
to cache fill with
a single request.

00:25:37.137 --> 00:25:39.220
The other thing is if you
do use an origin shield,

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:42.320
I recommend the
multi-regional storage class.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:44.290
The regional storage
class is actually

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:45.520
sometimes useful as well.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:47.950
Because you can place your
origin shield and your bucket

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.286
very, very close together.

00:25:49.286 --> 00:25:51.160
And that'll give you a
little tighter control

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:53.070
over your latency when
there is a cache miss.

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:55.153
It's a little tighter
control of your tail agency.

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:58.860
OK.

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.390
Third section is going
to be on write scaling.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.930
So this is a common
pattern that we see.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.590
We see folks who
are writing objects.

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:08.214
And maybe they're naming the
objects after the current time.

00:26:08.214 --> 00:26:09.630
And they're writing
them in order.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.046
So in this particular case, I
have a whole bunch of jpegs.

00:26:12.046 --> 00:26:15.110
And maybe there's been
time stamped by my camera.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.870
And often what we see
is when this happens,

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:21.736
people are able to scale
the write rate for a while.

00:26:21.736 --> 00:26:23.610
And then all of a sudden,
they hit this wall.

00:26:23.610 --> 00:26:27.580
And they can't seem to get over
a certain writes per second.

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:30.734
So what's going on here?

00:26:30.734 --> 00:26:33.150
To kind of understand, we
should actually take a step back

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:34.920
and see how GCS was designed.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:38.970
And we start off with
a few requirements.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.940
Back in the day, we wanted to
be able to read and write data,

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.950
and perhaps do that quickly.

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:45.325
And you know,
that's not too bad.

00:26:48.239 --> 00:26:50.030
And then you might add
another requirement.

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:51.405
Which is hey, you
should probably

00:26:51.405 --> 00:26:53.480
be resilient to power
failure or hardware

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:54.700
failure or human failure.

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.450
So things are getting
a little bit harder.

00:26:56.450 --> 00:27:00.530
But still kind of all in all,
I think we can deal with this.

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:04.940
We can use journaling to recover
from power failure, replication

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.700
to handle hardware
failure, and all sorts

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:10.880
of different techniques
for human failure backup.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:14.000
Slow rollouts, automation
testing, that sort of thing.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.550
So we can take care
of this as well.

00:27:18.554 --> 00:27:19.970
And then maybe a
third requirement

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:22.820
was hey we should probably
scale to lots of data.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:26.030
And still at this
point, it's not too bad.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:28.404
We can use essentially
a distributed hash.

00:27:28.404 --> 00:27:29.570
And this scales pretty well.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:34.060
We distribute our objects pretty
evenly across all of our nodes.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:35.810
We can still do things
relatively quickly.

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:37.940
You hash your
object, you quickly

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:40.400
find which node owns it, and
you go directly to that node.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:42.066
There's not a lot of
intermediate layers

00:27:42.066 --> 00:27:45.320
there to cause scaling
bottlenecks or performance

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:48.020
bottlenecks.

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:50.600
And I kind of underplayed
this because actually there's

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:52.552
a lot of smart folks
who have done this

00:27:52.552 --> 00:27:54.260
and we're lucky to be
able to rely on it.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.760
But this was actually how one
of the initial versions of GCS

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.460
was built internally.

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:01.670
We were essentially this
big distributed hash.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:08.606
So unfortunately, folks
came along and said, hey,

00:28:08.606 --> 00:28:10.480
I'd be really nice if
we could list our data.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:14.020
And we want to be able to
list our data in order.

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:15.130
We want it to be accurate.

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:18.220
Which means we talked about
strong list consistency.

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:20.680
That's been one of the large
requests that we've had.

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:22.430
And we want to be able
to do that quickly.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:24.550
And so it's this
last requirement

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:26.610
that really is problematic.

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:29.120
The distributed hash approach
really doesn't work now.

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:31.300
There's no affinity
between objects

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:32.740
that have similar names.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:34.700
And so in order for
you to list in order,

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:36.880
you essentially have to
query every single note.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.700
If a single node fails,
your entire listing fails.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:41.950
If a single node is slow,
your entire listing is slow.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:42.800
Right?

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:46.539
So this essentially
forces us to do sharding

00:28:46.539 --> 00:28:47.580
a little bit differently.

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:51.610
Where we shard objects into
lexicographical ranges.

00:28:51.610 --> 00:28:55.250
So in this example,
I've got nine ranges.

00:28:55.250 --> 00:29:01.000
The first shard is any object
whose name comes before 05.jpg.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.610
And the second shard is any
object whose name comes between

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:07.230
05.jpg and 22.jpg.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:09.540
And what GCS does is
it takes the shards

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.270
and it distributes
them to machines.

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:14.830
And here's an example,
we have four machines

00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:16.077
and we distribute the shards.

00:29:16.077 --> 00:29:17.410
We, of course, have replication.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:20.400
So you can imagine
having different zones

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:21.789
with replicas of these shards.

00:29:21.789 --> 00:29:23.580
And we don't necessarily
have to distribute

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.500
the shards the same way.

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:28.850
But the key thing here is in
order for this to be effective,

00:29:28.850 --> 00:29:32.106
the amount of load that
shards 1, 2, and 3 receive

00:29:32.106 --> 00:29:33.980
has to be roughly equal
to the amount of load

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:35.190
that 4 and 5 receive.

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.860
And 6 and 7, and 8 and 9.

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:37.360
Right?

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:39.950
If we don't, then each of
these four machines in that

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:47.634
left region, they're not going
to have even load balancing.

00:29:47.634 --> 00:29:49.050
So the problem is
how do we decide

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:52.840
what these lexicographical
ranges should be.

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:55.140
So that we get this nice
even load balancing.

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:57.960
It's actually a little bit hard.

00:29:57.960 --> 00:29:59.850
Primarily because
we don't really

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.320
control what the
object naming is.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:03.690
And we don't really
control the access pattern

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:06.600
that our users use when
they access their objects.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.630
So here's one example
of a potential load

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.550
versus object names.

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:14.100
And here's another very
different example, right?

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:16.650
In that second
example, this could

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:19.920
be someone who's writing to two
subdirectories, foo and bar.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.080
And not writing anything
else outside of that.

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:23.895
And so only around
foo and around bar

00:30:23.895 --> 00:30:25.880
do you see these spikes in load.

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.950
And so for that, we would
of course split the ranges

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:29.797
within foo and bar.

00:30:29.797 --> 00:30:32.130
And for this other one, we
would have probably much more

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.610
even splits.

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:38.280
So one of the key things
here that I want to point out

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:41.040
is we need a little bit help.

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.770
We have to really look
at your past behavior

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.690
to try to figure out
how to shard things.

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:47.100
And we will do that, right?

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:49.140
If you maintain a
relatively consistent load,

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:49.974
we'll figure it out.

00:30:49.974 --> 00:30:51.890
We'll split things into
the appropriate shards

00:30:51.890 --> 00:30:52.646
and redistribute.

00:30:52.646 --> 00:30:54.270
But if you're constantly
moving around,

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:56.850
then it makes it very
hard for us to react.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:01.930
Let's go back to in-order
writes for a second

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:03.470
and see why these
are problematic.

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:06.520
So if we start out, let's
imagine we're writing 00.jpg

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.775
through 99.jpg in order.

00:31:08.775 --> 00:31:10.150
And we'll start
off with the base

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:11.566
where we have just
a single shard.

00:31:11.566 --> 00:31:14.890
That means only a single machine
can handle these requests.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:17.650
So we're writing these in
order for machine number one.

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:20.392
And of course, if we wanted
to use a second machine,

00:31:20.392 --> 00:31:21.850
that's not going
be doing anything.

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:24.520
There's not a shard that we
can assign to that machine.

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:27.604
And so with this current set
up, the amount of write scaling

00:31:27.604 --> 00:31:29.020
you can get is
going to be limited

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.580
by the capacity of
a single machine.

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:35.890
So one of the things we
might think to do is let's

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:36.970
split up our shards.

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:38.160
Let's have two shards.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.302
0 through 50, 50 through 99.

00:31:40.302 --> 00:31:42.010
And let's distribute
each of these shards

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:43.840
to a different machine.

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:47.560
So what happens with
in-order writes?

00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:51.640
First of all, you start writing
00, 01, all the way through 49.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:53.440
All of those hit
that first shard.

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:55.524
All of those end up
on machine number one.

00:31:55.524 --> 00:31:57.190
And while machine
number one is handling

00:31:57.190 --> 00:31:58.000
all these writes, machine.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:31:59.200
Number two is
sitting there idle.

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:00.320
Not doing anything at all.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:01.610
Right?

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:05.499
And then you start writing
50.jpg, 51.jpg through 99.jpg.

00:32:05.499 --> 00:32:07.540
And these all of us are
hitting the second shard,

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:09.562
which is hosted on
machine number two.

00:32:09.562 --> 00:32:12.020
And since all the requests are
going to machine number two,

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:13.970
machine number one is idle.

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:16.670
So at any given point in time,
only one machine is active.

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:20.390
And your total write capacity is
limited by that single machine.

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:23.152
So this particular
strategy hasn't really

00:32:23.152 --> 00:32:25.610
bought us anything in terms of
our ability to scale writes.

00:32:28.986 --> 00:32:30.360
We could take this
to the extreme

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:33.330
and use a ton of shards.

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:36.720
In this example, we have
a bunch of shards, one

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:39.450
essentially for
every single object.

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:41.670
And if we distribute
those shards alternatingly

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:45.880
between machine one
and two, we can indeed

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.950
use both machine one
and two simultaneously.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.660
And then we've doubled our
write throughput, right?

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:52.060
But this isn't really practical.

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:53.650
It doesn't scale well.

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:56.680
There's a lot of overhead in
terms of maintaining shards.

00:32:56.680 --> 00:32:58.120
And so that's one issue.

00:32:58.120 --> 00:32:59.620
And the other issue
is that we still

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:01.578
have to be able to predict
the fact that you're

00:33:01.578 --> 00:33:02.680
writing sequentially.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:04.870
And do this type of
splitting ahead of time.

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:06.500
We can't react after the fact.

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:09.140
We have to know up front
that this is happening.

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:12.940
So this is still
fairly problematic.

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:14.690
The solution here
is really to try

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:17.532
to change your write pattern.

00:33:17.532 --> 00:33:19.920
So for example,
rather than writing 0

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.720
through 99, just
sequentially, you

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:24.870
could go ahead and write
from two workers in parallel.

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:27.390
0 to 49, 50 through 99.

00:33:27.390 --> 00:33:30.000
And if you do that, then this
previous split into two shards

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.040
is actually pretty effective.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.624
You end up hitting both machines
one and two at the same time,

00:33:34.624 --> 00:33:36.623
you've essentially double
your write throughput.

00:33:36.623 --> 00:33:38.220
And if you do this,
by the way, GCS

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:40.590
will figure out that these
are the right ranges that

00:33:40.590 --> 00:33:42.270
should be used for the shards.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:45.510
It just takes a
little bit of time.

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:50.250
So to recap, some of the best
practices for write scaling

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.490
is for your steady
state load, make

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:55.770
sure you're pointing your
load at a consistent range

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:56.770
of objects.

00:33:56.770 --> 00:33:58.770
If you're writing into,
say, a foo subdirectory,

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:00.360
keep it in the foo subdirectory.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:02.970
Over time, GCS is going to
figure out your write pattern.

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:04.740
It's going to split,
it's going to scale.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:07.890
If you suddenly shift over
to a different prefix,

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.070
we will again, you know,
react and split and scale.

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:10.900
But it will take some time.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:12.941
And you might see a little
bit of a blip in terms

00:34:12.941 --> 00:34:14.219
of your write throughput.

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:15.690
If you're doing a one
off write where maybe you

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.190
don't have control
over the names

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.600
of your objects, parallelize.

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:20.100
Right?

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:23.040
Parallelize over
different ranges.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:27.062
This particular problem
doesn't affect everybody.

00:34:27.062 --> 00:34:28.770
Roughly, you can expect
to be able to get

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:30.570
a low thousands of
writes per second

00:34:30.570 --> 00:34:32.965
before you end up with any
write scaling bottlenecks.

00:34:32.965 --> 00:34:34.590
And we're working
pretty hard right now

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:36.263
to actually raise
that quite a bit.

00:34:36.263 --> 00:34:37.679
But just as a rough
rule of thumb,

00:34:37.679 --> 00:34:41.699
if you expect to need to
go over a few thousand

00:34:41.699 --> 00:34:43.620
writes per second,
start planning

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:45.570
for changing your
write access pattern

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:48.150
to avoid these bottlenecks.

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:51.090
And then finally in terms
of how quickly GCS reacts,

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:54.210
you want to think on the order
of low number of minutes.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:57.120
So again, it takes some time
to learn, some time to react.

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:59.970
So it's not maybe as
quick as we'd like.

00:35:05.995 --> 00:35:07.370
To get a little
bit more concrete

00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:08.780
in terms of what
you can do here,

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:10.640
I already mentioned
the idea that you

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:13.280
can upload from multiple
directories in parallel.

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:17.400
That's one way to really access
multiple object ranges at once.

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:19.820
Another thing you
can do is let's

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:21.980
say you're writing a bunch
of objects sequentially.

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:25.670
So in this case, 0005123.jpg.

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:27.590
You can actually hash
that name and insert

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:29.150
the prefix of the hash.

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:32.840
And by doing that, since your
hash function is creating

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:35.630
hash values that should
be uniform over the range

00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:38.510
of hash values, you're
distributing your load.

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:40.730
And you're allowing GCS
to go ahead and create

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:43.152
splits that are effective.

00:35:43.152 --> 00:35:45.110
And you don't necessarily
have to put this hash

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:46.859
at the very beginning
of your object name.

00:35:46.859 --> 00:35:48.770
So in this example,
we inserted the hash

00:35:48.770 --> 00:35:50.249
after the foo directory.

00:35:50.249 --> 00:35:52.790
And that means that any rights
that go into the foo directory

00:35:52.790 --> 00:35:53.600
are going to be split.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:55.558
And they're going to
scale really, really well.

00:35:55.558 --> 00:35:56.990
And so that'll work just fine.

00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:58.820
Again, as long as
you don't suddenly

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:02.090
shift your traffic from foo to
a different prefix like bar.

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:06.690
OK.

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:08.010
So that's write scaling.

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:10.710
To wrap this up, I wanted
to talk about retries a bit.

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:16.880
So here is a typical
retry strategy.

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:18.370
It should look pretty familiar.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:19.540
You get an error.

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:21.682
You do exponential back
off, you add jitter.

00:36:21.682 --> 00:36:22.390
Things are great.

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:28.310
So one question that
kind of comes up

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:32.734
is what's an appropriate
timeout when I call GCS.

00:36:32.734 --> 00:36:34.400
You might see something
on the short end

00:36:34.400 --> 00:36:35.437
like 500 milliseconds.

00:36:35.437 --> 00:36:37.520
You might see something
on the long end, something

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:38.720
like 10 seconds.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:40.690
So what's a good timeout?

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:43.090
It's tough because there's
a little bit of a trade off.

00:36:43.090 --> 00:36:47.470
If you use a short timeout,
then what's really nice

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:51.610
is that if you happen to hit
a unlucky slow machine that's

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:53.840
maybe temporarily overloaded,
you retry quickly.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:56.390
And so you control your
tail latency pretty well.

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:59.530
However, if system wide,
we're seeing a slow down,

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:01.570
then you might be
prematurely giving up.

00:37:01.570 --> 00:37:03.850
Maybe if you had waited just
a second or two seconds,

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:04.750
your request would complete.

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:06.625
And now all of a sudden,
all of your requests

00:37:06.625 --> 00:37:09.430
are failing because you've
set this really short 500

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:10.720
millisecond timeout.

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:15.150
It also ends up wasting a bunch
of work on the server's side.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.580
On the flip side, if you
set a really long timeout,

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:20.590
then you're resilient to
the system wide slowdowns.

00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:24.330
But you're now susceptible
to tail latency.

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:26.469
Again, if I hit that
unlucky machine,

00:37:26.469 --> 00:37:29.010
I'm waiting around potentially
for 10 seconds before I retry.

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:30.890
And if I'd simply
retried faster,

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:33.010
I could have got back to
the user a lot quicker.

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:34.951
Right?

00:37:34.951 --> 00:37:36.950
And one of the things I
actually want to mention

00:37:36.950 --> 00:37:40.160
is that tail latency is
sometimes a bigger problem

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:41.670
than you might imagine.

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:44.859
So for example, let's say
we're measuring tail latency

00:37:44.859 --> 00:37:45.650
at 99th percentile.

00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:50.240
So one out of every 100 requests
is taking, say, one second.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.432
And for any given user
visible operation,

00:37:53.432 --> 00:37:55.890
you might be doing a whole
bunch of lower level operations.

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:57.556
So in this particular
case, let's assume

00:37:57.556 --> 00:38:00.030
you're making 50
parallel reads to GCS.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:01.530
And you need to
wait for all of them

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:06.187
to complete before you
consider your job done.

00:38:06.187 --> 00:38:08.145
And by the way, again,
this doesn't necessarily

00:38:08.145 --> 00:38:08.760
have to be GCS.

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:10.218
You can imagine
loading a web page,

00:38:10.218 --> 00:38:11.870
you're loading multiple assets.

00:38:11.870 --> 00:38:13.620
Basically, there's a
lot of sub operations

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:15.940
that really have to
complete before you're done.

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:19.270
Well unfortunately, if
there is a 99% chance

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:23.170
that any particular request
succeeds in one second,

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:29.050
then there's 0.99 raised to
the 50th chance that all 50

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:32.024
of those requests are going
to succeed within one second.

00:38:32.024 --> 00:38:33.190
And that's surprisingly low.

00:38:33.190 --> 00:38:34.924
It's less than 61%.

00:38:34.924 --> 00:38:36.340
So all of a sudden,
you're talking

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:39.310
about a property that
really only affects one out

00:38:39.310 --> 00:38:40.720
of 100 requests.

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:43.930
It's causing over a third
of your jobs to be slow.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:44.680
Right?

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:45.910
So it can be surprising.

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:49.750
Like tail latency can have a
bigger impact on median latency

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:53.960
and on your overall
latency once you step up.

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:58.220
So one of the strategies you can
use for addressing tail latency

00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:00.260
is something called
hedge requests.

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:02.730
So the idea here is let's
say we start an attempt,

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:03.620
we make an RPC.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:04.940
And it's taking a while--

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:08.490
it hasn't come back yet.

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:11.520
We can designate
some type of timeout.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:14.280
And I'll go into more detail
like what a good timeout is.

00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:16.080
But for now, let's
suppose we wait

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.400
until are expected 95th
percentile latency.

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:22.130
And we still haven't
gotten a response yet.

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:25.332
At this point, you can actually
submit a second attempt.

00:39:25.332 --> 00:39:26.790
You don't cancel
the first attempt,

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:30.170
you don't mark it as failed, you
just submit the second attempt.

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:34.010
And because we've chosen 95th
percentile latency as our cut

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:36.650
off, in theory this
should only generate

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:38.810
about 5% extra traffic.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.250
So it's manageable.

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:41.500
Right?

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:44.920
Now, if this were a
case where we simply

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.500
were hitting an unlucky
machine, an unhealthy machine,

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:52.120
and that's it, it's not a system
wide issue, then this retry,

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:54.280
chances are it's going
to hit a healthy machine.

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:55.570
And it's going to
complete pretty quickly.

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:58.000
Because typically, your
median or your common latency

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.800
is much, much shorter
than your tail latency.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:03.200
So if that's the
case, then attempt

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:04.660
number two is going to succeed.

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:06.350
You can cancel
attempt number one.

00:40:06.350 --> 00:40:08.050
You can go ahead and
return the result

00:40:08.050 --> 00:40:10.030
from your second attempt.

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:11.770
Right?

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:15.890
What happens if we were in
system wide overload though?

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:18.710
Well, the nice thing with this
approach is that you never

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:20.360
actually cancelled
attempt number one.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:23.830
You don't use an
artificially low timeout.

00:40:23.830 --> 00:40:24.500
Right?

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:27.081
You kept, say, a reasonable
five or 10 second timeout.

00:40:27.081 --> 00:40:28.580
And so if the overall
system is slow

00:40:28.580 --> 00:40:31.401
and maybe all requests are
taking about two seconds,

00:40:31.401 --> 00:40:33.650
you can still wait for attempt
number one to complete.

00:40:33.650 --> 00:40:35.090
Or attempt number two.

00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:37.298
But in this particular case,
since attempt number one

00:40:37.298 --> 00:40:40.370
was started earlier, chances are
that it will complete sooner.

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:42.170
So once attempt
number one succeeds,

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.000
you again cancel
attempt number two.

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:47.395
And you go ahead and return the
result from attempt number one.

00:40:47.395 --> 00:40:48.770
So this is a head
retry strategy.

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:51.800
It's really nice-- in
this particular case,

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:54.080
we use the 95th
percentile as the cutoff.

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:58.160
You could expect that to impact
your tail latency at a longer

00:40:58.160 --> 00:40:58.890
tail.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:01.880
Right

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.880
So a couple of
notes around this.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.550
I mentioned that if we chose
a 95th percentile cut off,

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:10.910
we would generate about
5% extra retry traffic.

00:41:10.910 --> 00:41:12.730
That's not always true.

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:14.720
95th percentile latency
can change over time.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:15.350
Right?

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:17.924
So temporarily what you think
is 95th percentile latency--

00:41:17.924 --> 00:41:19.340
maybe the entire
system gets slow,

00:41:19.340 --> 00:41:21.780
and now you're retrying
every single request.

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:25.940
So it's safer to put a hard
cap in terms of how many hedged

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:27.080
requests you perform.

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:29.420
Maybe limit x percent
of your overall traffic.

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:31.024
Say 5% or 10%.

00:41:31.024 --> 00:41:33.440
And that way, when you're going
through these transitions,

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:35.231
you're not going to
generate a retry storm.

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:42.920
The other general recommendation
is in order to retry,

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:44.730
you really need to
have idempotence.

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:45.230
Right?

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:47.810
And this means that I
can perform an operation

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:49.010
multiple times.

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:51.750
And the end result is
going to be the same.

00:41:51.750 --> 00:41:56.600
And there's a couple of cases
where this is not obvious.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:58.840
Where a particular request
might be idempotent,

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:01.220
but a sequence of
requests isn't.

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:06.470
So for example, let's suppose
I write A to an object.

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:11.730
And then I follow it up and
I overwrite that with B. OK?

00:42:11.730 --> 00:42:14.020
Now let's suppose that first
attempt when I wrote A,

00:42:14.020 --> 00:42:16.202
it was being a little bit slow.

00:42:16.202 --> 00:42:17.910
So I made an attempt,
I tried to write A,

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:20.340
it's taken a long time,
so I go ahead and I retry.

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:22.180
And that second
attempt succeeds.

00:42:22.180 --> 00:42:25.860
So now we've written A.
I move on and I write B.

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:29.940
And unfortunately, that first
attempt to write A finishes up.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:30.720
What does it do?

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.720
It overwrites B.
And the net result

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.820
is I'm left with A. Right

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:38.040
And that's unexpected.

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:42.840
You would expect that slow
request to be dead and not

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:43.560
have any effect.

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:45.420
But it's actually
living in the system

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:47.790
somewhere and kind of
coming back to bite you.

00:42:47.790 --> 00:42:51.030
So there's a couple of
ways to work around this.

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:53.299
One obvious way is simply
don't overwrite objects.

00:42:53.299 --> 00:42:55.590
Anytime you write new content,
choose a different name.

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:56.830
If you do this, you're
not going to ever run

00:42:56.830 --> 00:42:58.350
into this type of issue.

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:00.390
You can also use preconditions.

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:04.500
For example, if I had
said write A if and only

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:09.330
if the object didn't
exist previously.

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:13.140
And then I can say it right
B only if the contents

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:15.780
of the object-- or the
version of the object was A.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:19.140
If you do this, then that
initial request to A is not

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:21.800
going to overwrite
B. Because B's--

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.430
the object exists at that point.

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:23.930
Right?

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:30.330
And then lastly, I just wanted
to point out exponential

00:43:30.330 --> 00:43:32.802
back off with jitter, like
the standard retry strategy.

00:43:32.802 --> 00:43:34.010
You should still be doing it.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:36.710
And really think
of it as focusing

00:43:36.710 --> 00:43:38.420
on when you get an error.

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:42.590
If I get an immediate 503 or 429
service telling me to back off

00:43:42.590 --> 00:43:44.000
or that it's
unhealthy, of course

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:46.420
you continue to do exponential
back off with jitter.

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:50.639
This hedge retry strategy,
in some ways it's orthogonal.

00:43:50.639 --> 00:43:52.430
It allows you to address
your tail latency.

00:43:56.191 --> 00:43:56.690
OK.

00:43:56.690 --> 00:43:59.790
So that's everything
we had today.

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:02.310
Just to quickly recap, we
covered four different areas--

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:04.500
data processing,
content serving,

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:07.200
write scaling, and retries.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:11.760
And be careful with the storage
classes that you choose.

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:14.340
Make sure you're
favoring larger requests,

00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:16.860
and you're parallelizing
as much as possible.

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:18.610
Make sure you're
turning on caching

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:21.990
so you can take advantage of
our built-in CDN functionality.

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:24.240
And then make sure
your write pattern

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:27.300
isn't going to generate
unexpected bottlenecks later

00:44:27.300 --> 00:44:28.870
on.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:31.680
And then finally, think about
using a hedge requests to cut

